11. TOPIC PREREQUISITES AND SEARCH ALGORITHMS

Create an interactive topic-prerequisite structure.

Allow topics to have prerequisite topics.

Display relationships using a graph or a clear interactive list.

Validate references and detect cycles. If cycles exist, explain why prerequisite-based planning may be invalid until they are resolved.

BFS and DFS

Implement BFS or DFS only for a meaningful operation, such as finding reachable prerequisite topics or traversing the topic graph.

If implemented:

Show the graph or relevant structure.

Display the traversal order.

Explain the algorithm.

Provide a small test graph with a hand-checkable result.

A* search

A* is optional. Include it only if a study-sequence planning feature is implemented.

Define:

Search state.

Legal actions.

Goal condition.

Action cost.

Heuristic.

Termination condition.

Use compatible units for g(n) and h(n). If cost is measured in study minutes, the heuristic must estimate remaining study minutes.

Do not add minutes directly to marks or percentages.

Do not claim optimality unless the algorithm's assumptions are satisfied.

Constraint satisfaction problem

CSP is optional. Use it only if the application genuinely schedules study sessions with variables, domains, and constraints.

Other algorithms

Do not add Minimax or alpha-beta pruning to the student-performance prediction workflow because these are designed for adversarial game search.

Do not add AO* simply to display another syllabus algorithm. Use it only if the application contains a genuine AND-OR planning problem.

Do not add the Expert System module. It is explicitly excluded from this project.

Classic water-jug, queens, travelling-salesperson, missionaries-and-cannibals, and tile problems should not appear as platform features unless a genuine project requirement is established.

Keep the core focus on student performance analysis and prediction.

